en=[
"A conventional program contains branches whose conditions a programmer can inspect. An AI workflow introduces a component whose interpretation of language is learned. The engineering problem is deciding where that interpretation is useful and which consequences remain governed by ordinary code. Replacing every if statement with a model call adds uncertainty to problems that already have exact answers. Keep arithmetic, authorization and explicit state transitions in code; use models where the input really needs interpretation.",
"Our running project is Patch Workbench: take a software issue, choose a reviewed skill, produce a candidate pricing function, validate it, assess its explanation, and return a draft for a maintainer. The requirement is deliberately small: discount_cents(price, percent) uses integer cents and rounds down. At 1,999 cents and 25 percent, the required result is 1,499 cents. A candidate that returns price minus percent is valid Python but solves the wrong problem. This one counterexample will travel through the course.",
"Use four precise names. A function transforms explicit values. A skill packages reviewed instructions and resources for a task. Jev supplies typed judgments about supplied state; a generative model supplies proposed code or prose. AI-Core is the architectural question of how much deployed work depends on model decisions and how much authority those decisions hold. Coding a deterministic service with an AI assistant does not by itself make that service AI-Core. This tutorial's draft-only tool is a bounded example for studying those responsibilities, not an empirical claim of model indispensability.",
"Start by writing a success contract: supported issue, authorized repository, allowed candidate language, correct observed behavior, useful explanation, and no external effect. The last condition is intentional: accepted_draft means ready for human review. It does not mean merged, deployed, or proven correct for every input. Anthropic's workflow/agent distinction supports choosing a fixed code path for this task [1]. Our decision to start with a narrow draft boundary is a course design choice. We will earn additional complexity only when a concrete failure makes it necessary."
],
