"A good programmer carries task knowledge in habits: where files belong, which tests matter, and what a reviewer expects. A skill makes some of that knowledge explicit and reusable. The Agent Skills specification defines a directory with SKILL.md containing metadata and instructions, with optional scripts, references and assets [1]. That packaging helps discovery. It does not automatically enforce permissions, test correctness or authenticate the author of a downloaded skill.",
"The companion includes skills/pricing-patch/SKILL.md and a corresponding host-side SKILL contract. It names the task, version, allowed path, function signature, evidence identifier and restricted arithmetic language. The host-side contract is authoritative for enforcement. Keeping prose and code separate makes a useful review question visible: do the instructions promise something the runtime does not enforce? In a larger registry, generate shared fields from one reviewed manifest and test the correspondence rather than relying on a maintainer's memory.",
"Separate selection from authorization. Jev may propose pricing, docs or unknown; the host only implements the reviewed pricing route in this small lab. A docs route therefore goes to review rather than being silently treated as pricing. This is a deliberate scope limit. The unknown route matters because forcing every issue into a known skill converts missing coverage into confident misrouting. TypeSafe's skill-suggestion cookbook demonstrates a shortlist and re-check approach [2]; our tiny catalog uses one narrow route and tests the unsupported path.",
"Pin the skill version and hash the contract in the run manifest. A changed instruction can change behavior even when the Python functions are unchanged. Review skill updates like dependency updates: provenance, diff, allowed resources and regression cases. Do not execute a skill's scripts simply because a model selected its name. The selected skill supplies a procedure; the application supplies capabilities. In the lab, the unknown decision returns review before any candidate is generated. That observable stop is the outcome we want readers to carry into a larger skill registry."
